      'New Issue Regulation New Issue Rule',
      'Initial public offerings must be sold to public investors fairly and must not give an advantage to industry insiders. FINRA\'s New Issue Rule is meant to protect the distribution of IPO securities from manipulation by securities industry insiders. In most cases, the New Issue Rule prohibits IPO securities from being distributed to any account in which a restricted person has a beneficial interest of 10% or more. Restricted persons include a variety of insiders, such as:\n\nBroker-dealers (whether involved in the IPO or not) and their employees\n\nPersons with a significant ownership interest in a broker-dealer (10% or more)\n\n• Attorneys, accountants, financial consultants, finders, or anyone else acting in a fiduciary capacity to the lead underwriter Portfolio managers buying for their own accounts (they may purchase IPO shares as normal for the accounts they manage)\n\nImmediate family members materially supported by a restricted person Underwriters: Underwriters and members of the selling group (and their agents) generally may not hold back shares of the IPO for their own accounts to sell later. However, underwriters may purchase unsold shares of an IPO under a standby agreement if the following conditions are met:\n\n• The prospectus discloses it.\n• The agreement is in writing.\n• A statement from the lead underwriter attests that it couldn\'t find any buyers.\n• The shares will not be sold within three months.\n\nIssuer-directed exemptions: Issuer-directed shares are shares the issuer designates for specific persons. The person that is sold the issuer-directed shares cannot be:\n\n• A broker-dealer\n\nBroker-dealer personnel (unless the restricted person or their family member is an employee or director of the issuer) A fiduciary of the managing underwriter A family member of one of these Anti-dilution provisions: The prohibition on sales of new issues to restricted persons does not apply to shareholders who want to maintain their proportional ownership of the company. Such persons are not restricted from purchases or sales of new issues, if they meet these conditions: • They have owned an equity interest in the issuer for at least one year prior to the offering. Their percentage of ownership will not increase above the amount owned as of three months prior to the filing of the registration statement. The purchase is not resold, transferred, or assigned for three months following the effective date of the offering.\n\nRegulation M\n\nRegulation M is a set of rules under the \'34 Act dealing with potential market manipulations during primary offerings. Trading by the underwriters is generally prohibited with one exception. Stabilization of a new issue in the secondary market is not considered to be manipulation. If a new issue price drops below its public offering price, the entire offering may be in jeopardy. Under certain circumstances, the managing underwriter is allowed to place stabilizing bids that will support the market price of the new issue. Stabilization rules require that certain conditions are met:\n\n• A notice of stabilization must appear on the inside front cover of the prospectus.\n• Only one stabilizing bid is allowed per market or market maker. The syndicate agreement will state that the syndicate manager is the sole firm that can make stabilizing transactions.\n\n• Astabilizing bid can only be placed at or below the public offering price. A stabilizing bid can never be placed above the public offering price.',
